mmunity's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {{\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {{\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            }},\n            {{\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }}\n        ]\n    }}\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n----Reports-----\ncommunity,full_content\r\n685,\"# ESPEN and Clinical Nutrition Community\n\nThe community is centered around the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN), which plays a pivotal role in advancing clinical nutrition practices across Europe. Key entities include various authors and organizations that contribute to the development of guidelines and protocols for nutritional support in diverse medical contexts, particularly for surgical and critically ill patients.\n\n## ESPEN's central role in clinical nutrition\n\nESPEN, the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism, is a leading organization dedicated to improving clinical nutrition practices across Europe. It develops comprehensive guidelines that address various aspects of nutritional support, including enteral and parenteral nutrition, which are essential for patient care in medical settings. The organization's emphasis on evidence-based practices ensures that healthcare professionals have access to the latest recommendations, thereby enhancing the quality of care provided to patients. ESPEN's influence extends to multiple patient populations, including those undergoing surgery, critically ill patients, and individuals with chronic conditions such as diabetes and liver disease [Data: Entities (380); Relationships (2918, 2921, 2932, 2941, 2956, +more)].\n\n## Collaboration with other organizations\n\nESPEN collaborates with various organizations, such as ASPEN and the Canadian Critical Care Practice Guidelines, to establish international standards for nutritional practices. This collaboration enhances the dissemination of guidelines and recommendations, ensuring that they are relevant and applicable across different healthcare contexts. The partnership with ASPEN, in particular, highlights the shared commitment to improving nutritional support for patients, which is crucial for optimizing recovery outcomes in surgical and critically ill populations. Such collaborations also facilitate the exchange of research findings and best practices, further strengthening the community's impact on clinical nutrition [Data: Relationships (2941, 2956, 2984, 2985, 2986, +more)].\n\n## Focus on malnutrition and nutritional screening\n\nESPEN places significant emphasis on addressing malnutrition, particularly in vulnerable populations such as the elderly and critically ill patients. The organization recommends various screening tools, including the Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST) and the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA), to identify individuals at risk of malnutrition. By promoting the use of these tools, ESPEN aims to facilitate timely interventions that can improve patient outcomes and reduce the prevalence of malnutrition in healthcare settings. This proactive approach is essential for enhancing the overall quality of care and ensuring that patients receive appropriate nutritional support [Data: Relationships (2925, 2939, 2943, 2947, 2953, +more)].\n\n## Guidelines for specific patient populations\n\nESPEN develops tailored guidelines for various patient populations, including those with specific conditions such as cancer, liver disease, and diabetes. These guidelines provide healthcare professionals with evidence-based recommendations for nutritional interventions that address the unique needs of these patients. For instance, ESPEN's guidelines for cancer patients emphasize the importance of nutritional support in improving treatment outcomes and quality of life. By focusing on the specific requirements of different patient groups, ESPEN ensures that nutritional care is both effective and relevant [Data: Relationships (2933, 2917, 2922, 2949, 2951, +more)].\n\n## Impact of Nutrition Day initiative\n\nNutrition Day, organized by ESPEN, is an initiative aimed at evaluating the nutritional status of patients admitted to hospitals across Europe. This initiative raises awareness about the prevalence of malnutrition and its implications for patient care. By collecting data on nutritional practices and outcomes, Nutrition Day helps to inform healthcare providers about the effectiveness of their interventions and highlights areas for improvement. The initiative's findings contribute to the ongoing efforts to combat malnutrition in healthcare settings, reinforcing ESPEN's commitment to enhancing nutritional care [Data: Entities (8074); Relationships (2966, 2946, 16970, +more)].\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n603,ASPEN,\"The American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) is a professional organization dedicated to providing comprehensive guidelines and recommendations for nutritional support in clinical settings, particularly focusing on parenteral and enteral nutrition. ASPEN emphasizes the importance of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) as the gold standard for nutritional evaluation and offers guidance on the screening and management of malnutrition, especially in oncology patients.\n\nASPEN develops clinical practice guidelines that address the nutritional needs of various patient populations, including those in critical care, surgical patients, and individuals with traumatic injuries. The organization has coordinated efforts with the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) to enhance the quality of nutritional care. It also provides specific recommendations for managing nutritional support in critically ill patients, including maintaining moderate blood glucose levels in ICU settings.\n\nIn addition to its focus on surgical patients, ASPEN has published guidelines for nutritional support post-surgery and has made modifications to commercial formulations of trace elements to prevent deficiencies in parenteral nutrition. The organization promotes standardized definitions and guidelines for nutritional care, ensuring that healthcare providers have the necessary resources to support patients with inadequate oral intake or those who cannot tolerate enteral nutrition.\n\nASPEN's commitment to evidence-based nutrition is reflected in its history, having published its first clinical practice guidelines in 1993. Overall, ASPEN plays a crucial role in advancing the field of nutrition, particularly in the context of parenteral and enteral nutrition, by providing essential guidelines and resources for healthcare professionals.\",41\r\n12869,NUTRIC SCORE,\"The NUTRIC SCORE is a comprehensive scoring system designed to assess the nutritional risk of critically ill patients, particularly in critical care settings. It serves as a valuable tool for quantifying the risk of these patients developing adverse events and helps identify individuals who would benefit from optimized nutritional support. The NUTRIC Score utilizes an online calculator to evaluate various health parameters, providing a scoring system that indicates the risk of poor clinical outcomes. Additionally, it assesses the need for aggressive nutritional intervention, ensuring that healthcare providers can make informed decisions regarding nutritional care for critically ill patients. Overall, the NUTRIC SCORE is essential for improving patient outcomes through targeted nutritional assessment and intervention.\",14\r\n9324,CALORIES,\"CALORIES refer to a measure of energy provided by food and nutritional products, which is essential for individuals requiring additional energy intake. Managing caloric intake is particularly crucial for individuals with diabetes, as it plays a significant role in their health management across different life stages, including pregnancy and old age. Additionally, CALORIES are the focus of a multicentre, randomised controlled trial that compares the clinical effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of early nutritional support delivered via the parenteral route versus the enteral route in critically ill patients. This trial aims to enhance understanding of how caloric support can be optimized in critical care settings.\",8\r\n12624,GUIDELINES,\"GUIDELINES are systematic recommendations established by ASPEN to provide nutritional support for surgical patients. These guidelines serve as protocols aimed at improving patient care and outcomes, particularly focusing on the prevention of complications in transplant recipients. They are designed to assist healthcare professionals in making informed decisions about patient care based on evidence, ensuring that the best practices are followed in various clinical settings.\",4\r\n1204,NUTRITIONAL RISK SCREENING,\"Nutritional Risk Screening refers to a systematic approach aimed at assessing the risk of malnutrition, particularly among older adults and patients in various healthcare settings. The Nutritional Risk Screening (NRS 2002) is a specific tool designed for this purpose, which helps identify individuals at risk of malnutrition, guiding healthcare professionals in implementing appropriate interventions to improve patient outcomes. This tool is especially relevant in hospitalized environments and is recommended for use in patients entering the Intensive Care Unit (ICU). A systematic review of nutritional screening tools highlights the importance of these assessments in predicting mortality and functional decline in older adults, underscoring the critical role of Nutritional Risk Screening in enhancing patient care and management.\",8\r\n554,BAPEN,\"The British Association for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (BAPEN) is an organization dedicated to providing guidance and support for nutritional care in the UK. BAPEN focuses on nutrition support and has developed guidelines specifically for malnutrition screening. Additionally, the organization oversees practices and guidelines related to artificial nutrition, ensuring that standards are maintained in the field of nutritional care.\",5\r\n16244,GRADE WORKING GROUP,The GRADE Working Group is an international collaboration that develops a system for rating the quality of evidence and strength of recommendations in healthcare.,5\r\n16220,AGREE,AGREE is a collaboration initiated in 1998 among several countries aimed at improving 